From 154bc9681f44fe428d1714aaf3f7708fe9891a23 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dag Sommer <dagaleks@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 14:28:21 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 08/34] windows: write the AP idle trampoline, and stop reading
 HLT as shutdown

Two bugs that would have shown up the moment a Windows guest was given more
than one vCPU.

`AP_TRAMPOLINE_START` was documented in the x86_64 layout as "the address of
the AP idle trampoline (`cli; hlt; jmp` loop)" and `setup_regs` already parked
every non-boot vCPU's RIP there -- but nothing ever wrote those instructions.
An AP would have started executing zeroed guest memory (a run of
`add [bx+si], al`) and wandered into a triple fault. `setup_ap_trampoline`
writes the four bytes, and `configure_x86_64` calls it before pointing any AP
at them. KVM needs no counterpart because it starts x86 APs in the
wait-for-SIPI state; WHP starts every virtual processor runnable, so the wait
has to exist as code.

The halt handling then had to change to match. It had followed the KVM
backend in treating a HLT with interrupts masked as a guest shutdown, which is
safe on KVM only because an in-kernel LAPIC means HLT never reaches userspace
at all. On WHP the AP trampoline *is* a `cli; hlt` loop, so that reading would
have torn the whole VM down as soon as a second vCPU started. HLT now always
parks. Shutdown continues to arrive through the i8042 reset port and
`exit_evt`, or as an unrecoverable exception.

x86_64-pc-windows-msvc: still 0 errors (also with --features blk).
x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu and aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu, --all-targets,
krun-vmm + libkrun: 0 errors. `cargo test -p krun-vmm --lib` is unchanged at
13 passed / 10 failed, the failures being pre-existing and all from /dev/kvm
being unavailable in this environment.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
